INT. GARAGE CANNING KITCHEN - AFTERNOON

Steam, vinegar, dill. Two folding tables of jars. A burner humming under a tall pot. The garage door is open on a sunny cul-de-sac.

FITZGERALD "FITZ" MULLIGAN, seventies, stirs the brine with a wooden paddle. His daughters, SIOBHAN, fifties, and ROXANNE, forties, work the packing line, and neither has looked at the other in an hour. Two grandchildren hover: KAI, twelve, with a stack of lids, and BEATA, nine, with a roll of labels.

SIOBHAN
Dad, the salt's wrong.

ROXANNE
It's fine.

SIOBHAN
It's too much, Rox.

FITZ
It's been this recipe since 1971.

ROXANNE
Siobhan counts every grain like it's evidence.

SIOBHAN
Because someone has to!

KAI
Can I put the lids on?

BEATA
I'm doing the labels, Kai.

FITZ
Everyone do their own thing.

SIOBHAN
Dad, you always say that!

Roxanne drops a jar. It shatters. Pickles skid across the floor. Kai freezes, Beata squeals, Siobhan lunges to stop the brine from spilling, Fitz sets down the paddle and turns off the burner.

FITZ
That's enough. Everybody out.

ROXANNE
Dad --

FITZ
Out. I'll finish.

They leave, one by one. He sits on an overturned crate among the pickles and, very quietly, begins to laugh.
